ase day and december of that same  year it would be the first time ever  that a mainline zelda game would receive  downloadable content so what does this  have to do with the sequel well jumping  forward a bit to june 18 2019 seven days  after the sequel's review at e3 producer  aj onuma explains in an interview with  kotaku that breath the wall 2 was  initially part of a series of ideas  meant for dlc but that it grew beyond  the scope of something a dlc pack could  handle it's hard to pinpoint the exact  moment the decision was made to turn the  dlc concept into a proper sequel but it  had to have been somewhere between the  launch of breath to wild and the second  dlc pack we know for a fact that the  zelda team was planning to make at least  one more dlc pack on top of the two we  ended up getting maybe even more than  that anuma explains that he wanted to do  more than just add new data to the base  game and so he opted to take the game's  engine and core assets and start from  scratch his choice of words of wanting  to start from scratch implies that the  sequel was always meant to be more than  just a simple asset flip with some new  enemies and dungeons thrown in there  which is a concern many fans have had  since the beginning anyway back to 2017.  on april the 4th one month after the  release of breath the wild the series  producer states in an interview with  famitsu that moving forward the open air  concept will likely be a standard for  zelda in the future which is not  necessarily a reference to breathtak 2  specifically as it's not clear if the  decision to start working on a sequel  was already made at this stage i'd say  probably not but for those who worry if  the sequel will have the same open  nature as its predecessor there you go  on june 30 2017 dlc pack 1 would become  available for download on both wii u and  switch and 5 months later on the 8th of  december aj onuma revealed dlc pack 2  the champion's ballot in front of a live  audience at 